// result_end

> Results

```
=== Sobel edge detection on a bright square ===
max gradient magnitude: 1081.87
interior of square (flat) magnitude at (16,16): 0
edge pixels (magnitude > 0.5*max): 124 (the square's outline)
```

The gradient magnitude is large only at the square's four sides and zero in its flat
interior, so thresholding it recovers the outline \-\- the edge pixels in the figure trace
exactly the boundary between bright and dark. That is the essence of gradient-based edge
detection: respond to change, ignore uniformity.


> Relatives and the Canny pipeline

Sobel is a first-derivative edge detector; the *Prewitt* and *Roberts* operators are
close cousins with different kernels. On its own Sobel gives a thick, thresholded edge map;
the *Canny* detector refines it into clean one-pixel edges by adding Gaussian smoothing,
non-maximum suppression (thinning), and hysteresis thresholding on top of the Sobel
gradient. Second-derivative detectors (Laplacian, Marr-Hildreth) instead find
zero-crossings. The Sobel gradient is the shared front end of most of them.

> Complexity

\(O(n)\) time for \(n\) pixels \-\- a fixed 3x3 convolution per pixel \-\- and \(O(n)\) memory
for the gradient images.
